1.186.m:
Bhāskara said:
On the full-moon day of Śrāvaṇa, an offering accompanied by mantras should be made to the serpents;
and ascending the bed should be performed by one desiring happiness.

1.187.m:
A gift offered with devotion that follows through one birth
is declared to be multiplied a thousand births for those destitute of muttering and sacrifice;
a gift to those living on muttering lasts until the dissolution of beings.

1.188.m:
Saptāśva said:
Having offered all cooked food to the Sun, one should offer it in fire;
having offered into fire, O hero, one should throw the offering in all directions around.

1.191.m:
he who is exceedingly pitiless towards servants, he who subdues cattle [cruelly],
he who listens intently to words spoken falsely,
he who betrays his master, friend, or guru, the deceitful, fickle, and rogue;

those who afflict [animals] with an excessive load and make them carry it without end;
and those other most sinful men who indeed crush the testicles of bulls
and make a barren cow work—those lowest of men are the most sinful;

1.195.m:
Saptāśvatilaka said:
Listen, O hero, best of birds, to the supreme rule of the saptamīs;
I shall proclaim all to you as you inquire properly.

1.196.m:
One should not weep with shedding of tears, nor contemplate piśācas;
one should not pull lice from the head, nor engage in idle talk;
one should avoid speaking ill of another and overbearing speech.
